```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0);
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
```--------------------```cs
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            Assert.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 ? Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount : true);
	    // @@@ If the return value is non-negative, size of old and new list is same 
        }
    }
}
```

This solution replaces the "PexObserve.ValueForViewing" calls with traditional Debug.Assert statements. The first set of assertions is checking that if the "LastIndexOf" method returns a non-negative value, then the size of the list before and after the operation should be the same.--------------------```cs
// @@@ If the return value is non-negative, size of old and new list is same 
Debug.Assert((New_Ret >= 0) == (Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount));
```

Please note that the actual nature of the assertion is dependent on the logic of the code. In this specific scenario, it is asserting whether the returned value is non-negative (indicating a valid position in the list) or not, only if the old and new list sizes are equal. If the asserted condition is false, it means an issue with the `LastIndexOf` method in the list implementation.--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0, "The return value is non-negative");
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount, "Size of old and new list is same");
```--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount);
```--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0);
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
```
The assertion statements based on the comments from the code:

1. `// @@@ If the return value is non-negative, size of old and new list is same` translates to `Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);`. It checks that if the return value `New_Ret` is non-negative and the old and new list sizes are the same.--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0); // If the return value is non-negative, size of old and new list is same 
```--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0, "If the return value is non-negative, size of old and new list is same");
```
This assert statement checks if the return value `New_Ret` is non-negative. If the condition is false, an error will be thrown with the message "If the return value is non-negative, size of old and new list is same". This assumes that the `LastIndexOf` method of the `ArrayList` class always returns a non-negative value if the element `x` is found in the list.
--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0); // If the return value is non-negative, size of old and new list is same 
```--------------------Here is the code for the assert statement corresponding to the comment "@@@ If the return value is non-negative, size of old and new list is same".

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
```

This assert statement checks if the return value (`New_Ret`) is non-negative (`New_Ret >= 0`) and whether the size of the old list (`Old_arrListCount`) is the same as the size of the new list (`New_arrListCount`). If these conditions are not met, the program will halt execution and possibly throw an exception.--------------------